Dreamline solves the machine-policy question: is this destination safe / within budget? The orthogonal question is: did a named human operator authorize *this specific payment* before it executed?

Those are two separate gates. An agent can satisfy Dreamline's policy check and still fire payments the operator never explicitly approved.

A minimal pre-authorization layer that sits above the Dreamline call:

```typescript
// payment_required — fires before x402 executes
{
  tool: "x402_payment",
  amount_usd: 0.05,
  destination: "https://api.example.com/paid-resource",
  agent_session: "eliza-session-abc",
  authorized_by: null,          // null = blocked until operator acts
  correlation_id: "pay-xyz-001"
}

// payment_approval — operator sets authorized_by
{ correlation_id: "pay-xyz-001", authorized_by: "operator:alice", approved: true }

// → now Dreamline /facilitator/verify runs
// → x402 payment executes

// payment_receipt — closes the loop
{
  correlation_id: "pay-xyz-001",
  authorized_by: "operator:alice",
  amount_usd: 0.05,
  destination: "https://api.example.com/paid-resource",
  tx_hash: "0xabc...",
  settled_at: "2026-03-29T00:00:00Z"
}
```

The two layers compose: Dreamline filters by policy, `payment_required` gates on named human authorization. Both can block the payment — for different reasons.

