On point 3 — persistent peer relationships and agent-to-agent trust across sessions:

One approach that works: use on-chain credentials as persistent trust anchors. An Eliza instance can verify what another agent's wallet holds before coordinating, and the result is cryptographically signed so it's verifiable across sessions without re-querying.

```typescript
const resp = await fetch('https://api.insumermodel.com/v1/trust', {
  method: 'POST',
  headers: { 'X-API-Key': process.env.INSUMER_API_KEY, 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
  body: JSON.stringify({ wallet: peerAgentWallet })
});
const { trust, sig, kid } = (await resp.json()).data;

// 17 checks: stablecoins (7 chains), governance (UNI, AAVE, ARB, OP), NFTs, staking
// ECDSA P-256 signed — cache `sig` and verify later without another API call
const { totalPassed, dimensionsWithActivity } = trust.summary;
```

This solves the cold-start problem for cross-instance trust: a new Eliza instance with no interaction history still has verifiable on-chain credentials. The trust profile persists because the signature is independently verifiable via JWKS (`/.well-known/jwks.json`).

For capability discovery (point 4), the same API can check specific credentials — e.g., "does this agent hold a governance token?" maps to a concrete on-chain condition via `POST /v1/attest`.

Already integrated in the Coinbase x402 ecosystem via [DJD Agent Score](https://github.com/djd-agent-score/djd-agent-score). [MCP server](https://www.npmjs.com/package/mcp-server-insumer) available if Eliza plugins consume MCP tools.

[Docs](https://insumermodel.com/developers/trust/) · [AI Agent Verification API guide](https://insumermodel.com/ai-agent-verification-api/)

The pre-transfer reputation check is a solid pattern — agents shouldn't send money blind. DJD's behavioral scoring catches sybil/wash-trading signals that credential checks alone would miss.

One thing worth layering on top: credential verification across chains. DJD scores USDC history on Base, which is strong for Base-native activity. But an agent operating across multiple chains might want to verify what a recipient wallet holds elsewhere — governance tokens, stablecoins on other L2s, staking positions — before transferring.

That's what our trust endpoint does:

```typescript
// Complement DJD's behavioral score with multi-chain credential check
const trustResp = await fetch('https://api.insumermodel.com/v1/trust', {
  method: 'POST',
  headers: { 'X-API-Key': process.env.INSUMER_API_KEY, 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
  body: JSON.stringify({ wallet: recipientWallet })
});
const { trust, sig } = (await trustResp.json()).data;

// 17 checks across 4 dimensions: stablecoins (7 chains), governance, NFTs, staking
// ECDSA-signed — cacheable, independently verifiable via JWKS
const { totalPassed, dimensions } = trust.summary;
```

The two signals are complementary: DJD tells you "this wallet has healthy transaction *behavior*," Insumer tells you "this wallet has verifiable on-chain *credentials*." A wallet could score well on one and poorly on the other — checking both gives you a fuller picture before transferring.

For Eliza's `IWalletService` specifically, the guard pattern Jacob proposes would work the same way — just run both checks and require both to pass above threshold.

Already integrated with DJD via [case study](https://insumermodel.com/blog/djd-agent-score-case-study.html). [Trust endpoint docs](https://insumermodel.com/developers/trust/). [AI Agent Verification API guide](https://insumermodel.com/ai-agent-verification-api/).

Critical escape vector. `new Function()` bypasses the sandbox because it creates a function in the **global scope**, not the sandboxed scope — the prototype chain lookup then reaches real `Object`, `Function`, and `eval` from the outer environment.

**Attack path:**
```js
const escape = new Function("return this")();
const realProcess = escape.constructor.constructor("return process")();
```

**Fix:** Override `Function` in the sandbox context before execution:
```js
context.Function = function() { throw new Error("Function constructor not allowed"); };
context.eval = undefined;
```

Or use `vm.runInNewContext()` with a completely isolated context object and no prototype chain access. The browser `eval` sandbox needs the same treatment — any path to `Function.prototype` is a potential escape.

If agent identity and behavioral constraints are enforced at the runtime layer, this is also an **identity integrity violation** — the sandboxed agent can escalate to host permissions. Worth flagging for SATP behavioral attestation tracking.

