## Static review + proposed minimal fix for the M-RoPE harness gate

On Metal M4 Max — no Intel Arc / NVIDIA box for the runtime repro from this issue body. Did a code-only review of `common/speculative.cpp` and `src/llama-batch.cpp` to nail down what's actually firing, and a proposed defensive fix that gets us out of the "0.3 t/s with per-batch error loop" footgun even without solving the deeper harness problem.

### What's actually happening (call-chain trace)

1. Spec-decode harness (`examples/speculative-simple/speculative-simple.cpp:217-222`) builds a target batch via `common_batch_add(batch_tgt, id_last, n_past++, { seq_id }, true)`. **`n_past` is a 1-D position counter**, so every drafted token gets a single int position passed to the batch.

2. `llama-graph.cpp:109-112` has the **runtime conversion path** for M-RoPE: when `ubatch->token && n_pos_per_embd == 4` it expands the 1-D `ubatch->pos` to a 4-row tensor by replicating the 1-D positions four times. So the compute graph itself handles the 1-D → 4-D widening fine.

3. The validation that fires in this issue's repro is in `src/llama-batch.cpp:265-273` (the `n_pos_per_embd > 1` branch). It does **not** complain about 1-D vs 4-D — it complains about position **ordering** across batches:

   ```cpp
   if (p0 >= 0 && p0 >= seq_pos_min(s)) {
       LLAMA_LOG_ERROR(... "for M-RoPE, it is required that the position satisfies: X < Y\n" ...);
       return false;
   }
   ```

   Where `p0` is `memory->seq_pos_max(s)` (last KV-cache position for this sequence) and `seq_pos_min(s)` is the minimum position in the new draft batch. The check asserts **strict** monotone advancement: every drafted token must start at `KV_max + 1` or later.

4. In the issue's trace (X=151, Y=146), the drafter proposed K=5 tokens at positions `[146, 147, 148, 149, 150]` — but the body's KV cache cursor is at 151. So `Y < X+1`, the check fires, the drafter batch is rejected, and the harness falls back to single-token re-decode.

### Why this only hits M-RoPE bodies

The non-M-RoPE branch at `llama-batch.cpp:289-321` uses the weaker `seq_pos_min(s) != p0 + 1` test — it allows positions to **be exactly equal** to the cache cursor (re-decode of the boundary token). The M-RoPE branch demands **strict** `X < Y`, presumably because the 4-D position state in M-RoPE bodies isn't safe to overwrite for re-decode.

When the target body has M-RoPE and the drafter's first proposed position lands at `KV_max - K + 1` (the typical spec-decode shape where the harness re-feeds the last accepted token alongside the K drafts), the boundary token's position equals `KV_max` — exactly the case the strict check rejects.

So this isn't actually about M-RoPE 4-D position propagation through the drafter — the math is fine. It's about the spec-decode harness's expected "re-feed the boundary token" pattern conflicting with the M-RoPE branch's strict-monotone position check.

### Two ways to fix it (both should be considered)

**(A) Reject the pair upfront — small, safe, lossy.** Add an M-RoPE check to `common_speculative_are_compatible` in `common/speculative.cpp`:

```cpp
static bool common_speculative_are_compatible(
    const llama_model * model_tgt,
    const llama_model * model_dft) {
    // ... existing vocab checks ...

    const llama_rope_type rope_tgt = llama_model_rope_type(model_tgt);
    if (rope_tgt == LLAMA_ROPE_TYPE_MROPE || rope_tgt == LLAMA_ROPE_TYPE_IMROPE) {
        LOG_WRN("%s: target model uses M-RoPE — speculative decoding is not "
                "currently supported for M-RoPE bodies due to a strict "
                "monotone-position requirement in src/llama-batch.cpp that "
                "rejects the harness's boundary-token re-feed pattern. "
                "See elizaOS/eliza#7631.\n", __func__);
        return false;
    }
    return true;
}
```

Effect: the spec-decode driver returns `false` from `common_speculative_is_compat`, the server logs `speculative decoding not supported by this context`, and the user gets body-only inference at ~3-4 t/s instead of 0.3 t/s with the per-batch error loop. Same UX as a non-spec-decode setup until the deeper fix lands.

**(B) Relax the M-RoPE check to match non-M-RoPE — actual fix, more risk.** Change `llama-batch.cpp:265` from `p0 >= seq_pos_min(s)` to `p0 + 1 < seq_pos_min(s)` so the boundary token re-feed succeeds. Mirrors the non-M-RoPE branch's tolerance. Requires verifying that M-RoPE bodies tolerate the re-feed (the 4-D position state for the boundary token may not be safe to overwrite — that's why the strict check exists).

I'd ship (A) as the immediate stop-the-bleeding patch and pursue (B) as the proper follow-up. (A) is one of the two paths the issue body proposed ("(b) reject the body+drafter pair upfront with a clear … diagnostic rather than degenerate to 0.3 t/s silently") and is < 10 lines. The cost is that no Qwen3.5/3.6 body can spec-decode until (B) lands, but **right now no M-RoPE body can spec-decode anyway** — (A) just makes the failure visible.

### Why I'm not pushing the patch from here

The fix lives in `common/speculative.cpp` in the [elizaOS/llama.cpp](https://github.com/elizaOS/llama.cpp) fork. Without Vulkan/CUDA hardware I can't verify that (A) actually changes the spec-decode init path from "silent 9.5% acceptance" to "clean rejection + body-only" rather than introducing a different failure mode (e.g., the harness asserting somewhere because spec-decode was disabled mid-flight). The 21-line SWA patch in [elizaOS/llama.cpp#3](https://github.com/elizaOS/llama.cpp/pull/3) had the same shape — small, mechanical, but needed someone with the hardware to confirm the runtime behavior matched the static reading.

Happy to land patch (A) on a `nubs/mrope-spec-decode-reject` branch if a maintainer with an Intel Arc / NVIDIA box wants to validate end-to-end. Otherwise this can wait for the same hardware pass that's already lined up for #7635.

### Adjacent eliza-side gating

Catalog gate from [#7629](https://github.com/elizaOS/eliza/issues/7629) just landed ([`d43b3517efa`](https://github.com/elizaOS/eliza/commit/d43b3517efa)). It adds `publishStatus: "pending"` per-tier. The right follow-on once (A) and the SWA fix from #7635 are runtime-validated is a per-tier `kernelCaps.unsupported` flag that the recommender consults to disable spec-decode wiring on tiers whose backend can't honor it yet — that closes the same loop this issue's "Suggested fixes" section #2/#3 describe ("Recommender guard: tag Qwen3.5/3.6 tiers with `requiresMRopeSpecDecode: true` … refuse to wire them through the spec-decode path until the harness supports M-RoPE.")

Leaving this issue open for the hardware-side runtime validation. The static reading of the failure path is complete and the proposed minimal fix is above.

